Discretization.

The first stage transforms the “continuous parameters” $(\Delta,\bm{A},\bm{B})$ to “discrete parameters” $(\overline{\bm{A}},\overline{\bm{B}})$ through fixed formulas $\overline{\bm{A}}=f_{A}(\Delta,\bm{A})$ and $\overline{\bm{B}}=f_{B}(\Delta,\bm{A},\bm{B})$, where the pair $(f_{A},f_{B})$ is called a discretization rule. Various rules can be used such as the zero-order hold (ZOH) defined in equation (4).

\overline{\bm{A}}=\exp(\Delta\bm{A})\qquad\overline{\bm{B}}=(\Delta\bm{A})^{-1}(\exp(\Delta\bm{A})-\bm{I})\cdot\Delta\bm{B}

